## Feature Engineering

This notebook focuses on transforming raw healthcare variables into meaningful features for predictive modelling, with a particular emphasis on identifying high-cost individuals.
import pandas as pd
import numpy as np

df = pd.read_csv("../data/processed/healthcare_clean.csv")
df.head()
threshold = df["charges"].quantile(0.9)
df["high_cost"] = (df["charges"] >= threshold).astype(int)
##Individuals whose healthcare charges fall within the top 10% are classified as high-cost individuals.
df["bmi_group"] = pd.cut(
    df["bmi"],
    bins=[0, 18.5, 25, 30, 100],
    labels=["underweight", "normal", "overweight", "obese"]
)
df["age_group"] = pd.cut(
    df["age"],
    bins=[18, 30, 45, 60, 100],
    labels=["young", "adult", "middle_aged", "senior"]
)
df["smoker"] = df["smoker"].map({"yes": 1, "no": 0})
df = pd.get_dummies(
    df,
    columns=["region", "bmi_group", "age_group"],
    drop_first=True
)
##One-hot encoding is applied to categorical variables for model compatibility.
X = df.drop(columns=["charges", "high_cost"])
y = df["high_cost"]

### Feature Engineering Summary
- Continuous variables were transformed to capture non-linear effects.
- Categorical variables were encoded using one-hot encoding.
- A binary target variable was constructed to represent high-cost individuals.
